    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>An <strong>electric elliptical under desk</strong> puts a small motor between you and the pedals. You plug it in, choose a speed on the remote, and the pedals glide in an oval path while you work, read or watch TV. You can let it move your legs or push along with it for more effort.</p>
<p>Electric models solve a real problem for people who find pedaling hard, but they also bring things a manual machine never has to think about: a power cord, a motor that makes some noise, controls that need to be within reach, and safety details like start speed and stop buttons. Those practical points decide whether you love the machine or return it.</p>
<p>This guide walks through what to check before you buy, how electric compares with manual, and four electric models worth considering, based on specs and owner reviews.</p>

<h2>Power and Cord Placement</h2>
<p>Every electric elliptical under desk needs mains power, usually through a plug-in adapter. Before you order, look under your desk and answer three questions.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Where is the nearest outlet?</strong> Ideally it is behind or beside the desk. Check the cord length on the listing and measure the route, not the straight-line distance.</li>
<li><strong>Where will the cord run?</strong> It should go from the back of the machine straight to the wall or along the desk leg, never across the area where your feet or chair wheels move. Cable clips or a cord cover keep it in place.</li>
<li><strong>Do you need an extension?</strong> If so, use a grounded, appropriately rated extension cord or power strip and keep it off the floor where possible. Avoid daisy-chaining strips.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> if you plan to move the machine between a desk and a sofa, set up a second cord route in each spot so you are not stretching a cable across the room. If moving it is a priority, a cordless manual machine may suit you better. See our <a href="/portable-elliptical-under-desk/">portable elliptical under desk</a> guide.</p></div>

<h2>Remote Controls and Speed Presets</h2>
<h3>Why the remote matters</h3>
<p>With the machine under your desk, the control panel is usually out of reach. A remote lets you start, stop and change speed without bending down mid-call. Look for a remote with clearly separated buttons, a dedicated stop button, and a way to keep it on the desk, such as a small stand or a spot next to your keyboard.</p>
<h3>Speed presets</h3>
<p>Electric models offer a range of preset speeds. More presets are not automatically better; what matters is the bottom and top of the range.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A slow lowest speed</strong> is essential for beginners, seniors and typing. If the slowest setting feels brisk, you will not use it during focused work.</li>
<li><strong>A useful top speed</strong> gives you somewhere to go once you are pedaling along with the motor.</li>
<li><strong>Forward and reverse</strong> lets you vary the movement. Reverse shifts some effort toward the calves and hamstrings.</li>
<li><strong>Timers and auto programs</strong> can switch speeds for you so you do not have to think about it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Check the listing for the exact number of speeds and the range, as these vary by model and change between versions.</p>

<h2>Motor Noise on an Electric Elliptical Under Desk</h2>
<p>Noise is the most common complaint about electric models. A manual magnetic machine makes almost no sound, while an electric one always has some motor hum, and it usually gets louder as speed rises.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Read owner reviews for "calls" or "meetings".</strong> Those comments tell you more than any marketing claim.</li>
<li><strong>Use a mat.</strong> A [[amazon:cubii-mat|non-slip mat]] absorbs vibration, which is often louder than the motor itself on hard floors.</li>
<li><strong>Run slower during calls.</strong> Most motors are quietest at their lower settings.</li>
<li><strong>Keep it tight.</strong> Rattles usually come from loose screws on the pedals or housing, not the motor.</li>
</ul>
<p>If silence matters more than powered motion, see our list of the <a href="/quietest-under-desk-elliptical/">quietest under desk ellipticals</a>.</p>

<h2>Safety Features to Look For</h2>
<p>A motor that moves your feet needs a few safeguards a manual machine does not.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Soft start.</strong> The pedals should ramp up gently instead of jumping to speed.</li>
<li><strong>Easy stop.</strong> A stop button on both the remote and the unit itself.</li>
<li><strong>Textured pedals or straps.</strong> These keep your feet from slipping off at higher speeds.</li>
<li><strong>A stable base.</strong> Rubber feet and enough weight to stay put when the motor is running.</li>
<li><strong>A stable chair.</strong> The motor pushes your feet, and your feet push the chair. Use caster cups, locking casters or a chair without wheels.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout warn"><p><strong>Caution:</strong> do not stand on a seated electric elliptical, keep pets and children away from the moving pedals, and unplug it when not in use. If you have a heart condition, neuropathy, a joint replacement or balance problems, check with your doctor before using a motorized machine.</p></div>

<h2>Electric Elliptical Under Desk vs Manual</h2>
